{\b Chapter 2 Literature Review}\par
\par
{\b 2.1 Introduction}\par
Micro-credentials have emerged as an important topic in higher education because they are increasingly associated with lifelong learning, flexible skills development, and employability. As universities, employers, and governments respond to rapid technological and labor-market change, interest in smaller and more targeted forms of certification has grown. However, the value of a micro-credential is not self-evident. Unlike traditional degrees, micro-credentials do not automatically benefit from long-established institutional meaning. Their value depends on whether learners regard them as credible, recognized, and worth the investment of time and effort.\par
\par
This chapter reviews the literature relevant to the study of recognition, stackability, and credential trust in IT micro-credentials in Thailand. The chapter begins by examining the concept of micro-credentials in higher education and their growing relevance to employability and lifelong learning. It then reviews the theoretical foundations of signaling theory and legitimacy, which together explain why visible features of a credential may shape students' evaluations. The chapter next discusses the major constructs used in the study, namely recognition, stackability, industry endorsement, credential trust, and perceived value. Finally, it synthesizes the literature and identifies the research gap addressed by this thesis.\par
\par
{\b 2.2 Micro-credentials in Higher Education}\par
\par
{\b 2.2.1 Concept and Characteristics of Micro-credentials}\par
Micro-credentials are generally understood as smaller, more focused, and more flexible certifications than traditional degree qualifications. Ahsan et al. (2023) describe them as competency-based credentials that often recognize specific knowledge or skills and may function as an additional, alternative, complementary, or component part of a formal qualification. This definition is important because it highlights the modular nature of micro-credentials. Rather than representing a broad course of study, they usually capture a narrower area of learning that can potentially stand alone or connect to larger educational pathways.\par
\par
Reed et al. (2024) similarly define micro-credentials as credentials that represent assessed achievement of a subset of learning at a modular level. Their work is especially useful because it emphasizes that micro-credentials should not be reduced to simple attendance certificates. A credential becomes meaningful when it is linked to evidence of learning and assessment. This distinction is central to the present study because credential trust is likely to depend not only on the short and flexible nature of a micro-credential but also on whether the credential appears to represent real achievement.\par
\par
The literature also suggests that micro-credentials are heterogeneous. Some are embedded within university structures, some are provided through online platforms, and others are associated with industry or professional partners. This diversity creates opportunity, but it also creates uncertainty. If micro-credentials vary in issuer, assessment practices, transcript visibility, and pathway value, then learners may need to rely on external cues when deciding whether a specific credential is worthwhile.\par
\par
{\b 2.2.2 Micro-credentials, Lifelong Learning, and Employability}\par
Micro-credentials are often discussed within the context of lifelong learning and employability. OECD (2021) argues that the quality and value of micro-credentials depend on transparency, trust, recognition, and clarity regarding what the credential certifies. OECD (2023) further notes that micro-credentials are increasingly promoted as tools for upskilling and reskilling because they may provide more flexible access to learning opportunities and faster responses to changing skills needs.\par
\par
The employability dimension of micro-credentials has received increasing scholarly attention. Zou et al. (2024) found that micro-credential learning has a positive relationship with students' perceived employability and that human capital plays a mediating role in this relationship. Their findings suggest that students may evaluate micro-credentials partly by the extent to which they believe these credentials can contribute to future career outcomes. Sharma et al. (2024) similarly argue that micro-credentials can augment employability when they are strategically integrated into higher education ecosystems. However, they also show that the effectiveness of micro-credentials depends on enabling conditions such as standardization, labor-market alignment, and institutional strategy.\par
\par
These studies are relevant to the present thesis because they indicate that students are unlikely to judge micro-credentials purely as short learning experiences. Instead, students may ask whether a credential is likely to improve future opportunities, signal useful competencies, or contribute to a broader educational and professional pathway. In that sense, micro-credentials are tied not only to learning flexibility but also to judgments about future value.\par
\par
The Thai context makes this discussion especially important. OECD (2025) highlights the importance of stronger lifelong learning systems, more adaptable skills development, and improved alignment between education and labor-market needs in Thailand. This policy context suggests that micro-credentials may become increasingly relevant to Thai students, particularly in fields such as IT where skills evolve rapidly. However, policy relevance does not guarantee student acceptance. The question remains whether students see such credentials as trustworthy and worth pursuing.\par
\par
{\b 2.2.3 Recognition, Quality Assurance, and Stakeholder Concerns}\par
Although micro-credentials are frequently presented as innovative and flexible, the literature also identifies a number of concerns relating to credibility, quality assurance, and recognition. Varadarajan et al. (2023) show that different stakeholders value different aspects of micro-credentials. Learners tend to seek flexibility, practical relevance, and timely skills acquisition, while employers want clear evidence of competencies. Higher education institutions are more likely to emphasize accreditation, trust, and integration into recognized systems. Governments often view micro-credentials as part of a larger skills and employability agenda.\par
\par
Raj et al. (2024) provide further evidence of these challenges by identifying major barriers to the implementation of micro-credentials as credible pathways to professional qualifications. Their study highlights problems such as unclear definitions, ambiguous course descriptions, lack of accreditation, and insufficient quality assurance. These issues are highly relevant because they show that micro-credentials do not automatically communicate legitimacy. If the meaning of a credential is unclear, students may have difficulty determining whether it is recognized and worth their investment.\par
\par
Employer-side evidence points in the same direction. Ha et al. (2024) found that employers generally recognized possible benefits of micro-credentials for employees, employers, and institutions, but they also reported low awareness and doubts about value as important barriers to effectiveness. This finding is significant because it suggests that questions of recognition and credibility are not limited to academic debates. They also operate in practical settings where micro-credentials are expected to create value.\par
\par
Overall, the literature indicates that the central challenge is no longer whether micro-credentials exist, but how their meaning is established. Recognition, quality assurance, and the ability to connect a credential to wider educational or labor-market pathways appear to be critical to how stakeholders understand their value.\par
\par
{\b 2.3 Theoretical Framework}\par
\par
{\b 2.3.1 Signaling Theory}\par
Signaling theory provides the principal theoretical foundation for this study. Spence (1973) developed signaling theory to explain how actors make decisions under conditions of uncertainty and information asymmetry. When one party cannot directly observe the underlying quality of another, visible signals become important. In labor markets, educational credentials function as signals because they provide observable evidence that may stand in for more difficult-to-observe qualities.\par
\par
This perspective is highly relevant to micro-credentials. Students cannot directly observe the long-term educational or labor-market value of a micro-credential before choosing to pursue it. Instead, they must rely on visible characteristics that suggest whether the credential is likely to be worthwhile. A credential that is formally recognized by a university, linked to credit accumulation, or endorsed by an industry body may be interpreted as more valuable than one without such features.\par
\par
Recent empirical work supports this signaling logic. Tee et al. (2024) show that learner responses to micro-credential programs are shaped by concrete program design factors. Zou et al. (2024) show that micro-credentials can influence perceived employability, which implies that learners interpret them partly as signs of future career relevance. Raj et al. (2024) show that unclear definitions and weak accreditation undermine the acceptance of micro-credentials as credible qualifications. Ha et al. (2024) similarly show that employer doubts over value can obstruct effectiveness. Together, these studies indicate that micro-credentials are not judged solely by their content. They are interpreted through visible signals of quality, recognition, and future payoff.\par
\par
In the present study, recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement are conceptualized as signals. They are visible characteristics that help students infer whether a micro-credential has institutional standing, future utility, and practical relevance. Signaling theory therefore provides a strong explanation for why these variables are likely to shape student evaluations.\par
\par
{\b 2.3.2 Legitimacy and Credential Trust}\par
While signaling theory explains how visible cues operate under uncertainty, legitimacy theory explains why some cues are accepted as appropriate and meaningful. Deephouse et al. (2017) define legitimacy as the perceived appropriateness of an organization or practice within a broader system of norms, values, and rules. Applied to micro-credentials, legitimacy concerns whether a credential appears to fit recognized standards of higher education, accepted assessment practices, and socially meaningful forms of certification.\par
\par
This is particularly important because the value of a micro-credential depends not only on what it represents, but also on whether people believe it deserves recognition. OECD (2021) repeatedly emphasizes the importance of trust, transparency, and recognition in the micro-credential ecosystem. Reed et al. (2024) show that faculty involved in micro-credential design place considerable emphasis on assessment quality and evidence of achievement. Raj et al. (2024) show that the absence of accreditation and quality assurance undermines credibility. Sharma et al. (2024) and Ha et al. (2024) reinforce this point by showing that standardization, integration, and stakeholder understanding are important to the success of micro-credentials.\par
\par
In this study, the more precise construct is credential trust, meaning students' confidence in the credibility, reliability, and institutional meaningfulness of the credential itself. Students may not describe their judgments in terms of institutional theory, but they still decide whether a credential seems credible, reliable, and worth believing in. Ohanian (1990) is useful in this respect because it offers a validated credibility framework centered on trustworthiness and expertise. In the present study, only these two dimensions are relevant; attractiveness is not used because the evaluated object is a credential rather than a human endorser. Although developed in the context of source credibility, its logic is relevant to the present thesis because students may form credibility judgments about a credential in much the same way that audiences form credibility judgments about a source. The combination of legitimacy theory and credibility logic therefore supports the view that recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement may influence perceived value partly by shaping credential trust.\par
\par
{\b 2.3.3 Integrated Theoretical Framework of the Study}\par
The theoretical framework of this study integrates signaling theory and legitimacy-trust logic to explain how students evaluate IT micro-credentials under uncertainty. Signaling theory explains why visible credential attributes matter at the point of judgment. When students cannot directly observe the actual future value of a micro-credential, they are likely to rely on visible cues such as formal recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement. Legitimacy and trust logic then explain how those cues are converted into judgments of credibility and institutional appropriateness.\par
\par
Within this framework, recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement are treated as external signal variables. Credential trust is treated as the central interpretive mechanism through which those signals affect judgment. Perceived value is treated as the final evaluative outcome because it captures whether students regard the credential as genuinely worthwhile relative to the effort required. In simple terms, the framework proposes that visible credential signals shape trust in the credential, and that trust shapes perceived value.\par
\par
This integrated theoretical framework is appropriate because micro-credentials do not yet carry the same taken-for-granted meaning as traditional degree qualifications. Their value often has to be inferred rather than assumed. The present study therefore uses the theoretical framework not to ask only whether students are interested in micro-credentials, but to explain how they judge the credibility and value of those credentials before any actual enrollment decision is made.\par
\par
{\b 2.4 Key Constructs in the Present Study}\par
\par
{\b 2.4.1 Recognition}\par
Recognition refers to whether a micro-credential is formally acknowledged by a university or another recognized educational structure. The literature consistently suggests that recognition matters because it reduces uncertainty about institutional standing and future use. OECD (2021) and OECD (2023) both stress the importance of recognition in establishing the value of micro-credentials. Varadarajan et al. (2023) show that institutions and employers expect clarity, trust, and integration, while Raj et al. (2024) show that the absence of accreditation and quality assurance creates barriers to acceptance. In the present study, recognition is treated as a visible signal that a micro-credential has formal meaning within an accepted educational system.\par
\par
{\b 2.4.2 Stackability}\par
Stackability refers to whether a micro-credential can count toward future formal academic credit within a recognized higher-education pathway rather than remaining a standalone certificate. This construct is grounded in the modular nature of micro-credentials described by Ahsan et al. (2023) and Reed et al. (2024). OECD (2023) also emphasizes the importance of pathways and flexibility in the use of micro-credentials for lifelong learning and employability. This more precise definition is important because the term stackability can otherwise be interpreted too broadly. In the present study, stackability is intentionally limited to pathway value within formal higher education. A stackable credential may therefore send a stronger signal of long-term usefulness because the effort invested in the credential can contribute to future educational progression.\par
\par
{\b 2.4.3 Industry Endorsement}\par
Industry endorsement refers to whether a micro-credential is co-developed and endorsed by a recognized IT industry association, employer consortium, or related professional body. This construct matters because it signals that a micro-credential has relevance beyond the university environment. Ha et al. (2024) provide direct evidence that employer understanding and perceptions influence how micro-credentials are valued. Sharma et al. (2024) and Zou et al. (2024) also support the broader employability logic that makes external relevance important to students. The present study uses this more specific definition to avoid treating endorsement as a vague label or as the reputation effect of a single well-known commercial brand. In this study, industry endorsement is treated as a cue that the credential has practical and labor-market value.\par
\par
{\b 2.4.4 Credential Trust}\par
Credential trust refers to the extent to which a micro-credential itself appears credible, dependable, and institutionally meaningful. It is conceptualized here as a judgment about the credential rather than a stable trait of the student or a general attitude toward a provider. The legitimacy literature suggests that trust is likely to depend on whether the credential aligns with accepted standards and recognized structures (Deephouse et al., 2017; OECD, 2021). Ohanian (1990) provides additional support for conceptualizing credibility in terms of trustworthiness and expertise, both of which are relevant to how students may evaluate the credibility of a credential. In this study, credential trust is expected to mediate the relationship between visible signals and perceived value.\par
\par
{\b 2.4.5 Perceived Value}\par
Perceived value is the primary outcome variable in this thesis. In higher education, perceived value is often understood as a trade-off between benefits received and sacrifices made. Alves (2010) provides a useful higher education perspective by arguing that perceived value can be measured through the relationship between price and quality together with comparison against alternatives. This approach is important because it highlights that value is not identical to usefulness alone. Rather, value reflects an overall judgment of whether what is gained justifies what is given up.\par
\par
Amado-Mateus et al. (2023) provide a more recent measurement-oriented contribution by evaluating the psychometric properties of a perceived value scale applied to universities. Their work is especially valuable because it offers an accessible route for operationalizing the construct in higher education. Together, Alves (2010) and Amado-Mateus et al. (2023) support the use of perceived value as a broader and stronger dependent variable than simple interest or behavioral intention. Tee et al. (2024) reinforce this interpretation by showing that learning experience influences engagement and willingness to pay, while Zou et al. (2024) show that students also consider future employability when judging the usefulness of micro-credential learning. Perceived value is therefore appropriate for the present study because it captures whether students regard a credential as genuinely worthwhile.\par
\par
{\b 2.5 Empirical Review and Research Gap}\par
The empirical literature on micro-credentials can be grouped into several broad themes. One theme concerns implementation, design, and stakeholder expectations. Ahsan et al. (2023), Varadarajan et al. (2023), Reed et al. (2024), Raj et al. (2024), Ha et al. (2024), and Sharma et al. (2024) all contribute to this line of work by showing that recognition, assessment, standardization, and integration are central to how micro-credentials function. A second theme concerns learner-side responses such as engagement, willingness to pay, intention, and continuance. Tee et al. (2024), Miao et al. (2024), and Wang et al. (2024) all fall into this category. A third theme concerns employability and future career outcomes, represented most clearly by Zou et al. (2024) and Sharma et al. (2024).\par
\par
Although this literature is valuable, an important gap remains. First, much of the student-side quantitative literature focuses on adoption, platform use, engagement, or continuance rather than on the legitimacy of the credential itself. Second, the literature contains useful discussions of recognition, accreditation, quality assurance, and labor-market relevance, but these factors are rarely combined into a single explanatory model centered on credential trust and perceived value. Third, the accessible studies are not focused on Thai IT students, despite the growing policy importance of flexible skills development and lifelong learning in Thailand (OECD, 2025). Finally, many existing studies ask about general perceptions of programs or learning experiences, whereas the present study focuses specifically on how concrete credential features shape judgments.\par
\par
The present thesis addresses this gap by applying an integrated theoretical framework in which recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement function as visible signals, credential trust functions as the mediating judgment, and perceived value functions as the primary outcome. This approach moves beyond basic adoption models by asking not simply whether students intend to use micro-credentials, but what makes a micro-credential appear credible and worth pursuing. It also offers a more focused explanation of how students evaluate micro-credentials in a specific national and disciplinary context.\par
\par
{\b 2.6 Chapter Summary}\par
This chapter has reviewed the literature relevant to the study of recognition, stackability, and credential trust in IT micro-credentials in Thailand. The literature shows that micro-credentials are modular and skills-focused credentials whose value depends on more than convenience. Recognition, assessment quality, pathway value, and stakeholder confidence all influence how these credentials are understood. The chapter also established the theoretical framework of the study. Signaling theory explains why visible cues such as recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement are likely to matter when students face uncertainty. Legitimacy theory and credibility logic explain why those cues may shape trust in the credential itself. Perceived value research further shows that students evaluate educational offerings through a trade-off between benefits and sacrifice, making perceived value a stronger outcome than simple intention or interest. Together, these strands of literature provide the theoretical and empirical foundation for the conceptual framework and hypotheses presented in the next chapter.\par
